I finally read Hyperion and belatedly realized that basically the entire “misaligned superintelligence” and p_doom literature is just repackaged Simmons. Interesting

I did this completely out of order by reading the contemporary Yudkowski/ Bostrom / Kokotajlo/Urban alignment literature and THEN reading the novels from the 80s that they’re basically all rewriting for modern audiences

Yes I’m deliberately trivializing their work by describing it as speculative science fiction because that’s what it is

@VentureCoinist There really is nothing to worry about I keep trying to tell people

@BradyDale I found it very frustrating to read because Simmons likes to drip out revelations and explanations VERY slowly and in the end I had to spend several hours with Chat getting to the bottom of everything (including fan theories to fill in mistakes and gaps)

people will say this of course but I havent come across a good sci fi treatment of covert superintelligent AI behaving exactly as the alignment crowd like to describe it - long term covert goal setting, treating humans as disposable, paperclip maximizing rather than malicious, humans dependent on AI/Core and thus blind to the Core's machinations, AI manipulating rather than conquering. if you know of fiction that better recapitulates the modern alignment discourse please share

